Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the portfolio project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Installed @vercel/analytics package
- Added `@vercel/analytics` to package.json dependencies
- Updated package-lock.json with the new package

### 2. Modified app/layout.tsx
- Imported the Analytics component from '@vercel/analytics/next'
- Added the <Analytics /> component to the root layout, placed alongside the existing <SpeedInsights /> component

### Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel Web Analytics quickstart guide (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), I:
1. Fetched the latest installation instructions from Vercel's documentation
2. Identified this is a Next.js App Router project (using TypeScript)
3. Installed the `@vercel/analytics` package using npm
4. Added the Analytics component import to the root layout file
5. Placed the <Analytics /> component inside the <body> tag, following the same pattern as the existing SpeedInsights component

The Analytics component is now positioned right before the SpeedInsights component in the body, which ensures it will track page views and other analytics events across the entire application.

### Verification Steps Completed

✓ Build completed successfully (npm run build)
✓ Linter passed with no errors (npm run lint)
✓ Package manager lock file (package-lock.json) properly updated

### Files Modified

- portfolio/app/layout.tsx - Added Analytics import and component
- portfolio/package.json - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- portfolio/package-lock.json - Updated with new dependency

### Notes

- The Analytics component follows the same integration pattern as the existing SpeedInsights component
- No environment variables are required for basic setup
- Analytics must be enabled in the Vercel dashboard under Analytics settings before deployment for data collection to work
- The component is framework-aware and will automatically track Next.js route changes
